# ============================================================
# CONTEXT COMPACTION ENGINE — SUPPORT DOCUMENT
# ============================================================
# Classification: INTERNAL — TRUSTED ENGINEERS ONLY
# System: Ardhanarishvara Infrastructure Layer
# Module: Context Compaction Engine (Y‑Prime Edition)
#
# This document describes the purpose, behavior, and safety
# responsibilities of the Context Compaction Engine. It is
# written in shell‑style format for compatibility with internal
# tooling and system scripts.
# ============================================================


# ============================================================
# SECTION 1 — OVERVIEW
# ============================================================
# The Context Compaction Engine prevents context overflow in
# multi‑agent LLM systems by reducing accumulated conversational
# history when token limits are exceeded.
#
# It ensures:
#   - predictable memory usage
#   - stable inference performance
#   - safe long‑running conversations
#   - deterministic compaction behavior
#
# All AI entities (Arka, Astra, Aruhan, Arkastra, Lux, Disturition)
# rely on this engine to maintain safe context boundaries.


# ============================================================
# SECTION 2 — PURPOSE & RESPONSIBILITIES
# ============================================================
# PRIMARY RESPONSIBILITIES:
#
#   1. Monitor total context token count.
#   2. Detect when context exceeds safe thresholds.
#   3. Remove low‑priority or older blocks of context.
#   4. Preserve essential reasoning segments.
#   5. Maintain deterministic compaction behavior.
#
# Without this engine, the system risks:
#   - VRAM overload
#   - inference slowdown
#   - context corruption
#   - model instability


# ============================================================
# SECTION 3 — THRESHOLDS & LIMITS
# ============================================================
# max_tokens:
#   - The absolute maximum allowed context size.
#   - Default: 32000 tokens.
#
# min_retain:
#   - Minimum tokens to preserve after compaction.
#   - Default: 8000 tokens.
#
# aggressive_cut:
#   - Percentage of context to remove during compaction.
#   - Default: 0.40 (40%).
#
# These values should be tuned based on hardware and model size.


# ============================================================
# SECTION 4 — COMPACTION LOGIC
# ============================================================
# The engine performs compaction when:
#   total_tokens > max_tokens
#
# COMPACTION STRATEGY:
#   - Remove oldest blocks first.
#   - Continue removing until the target reduction is met.
#   - Preserve the most recent and most relevant blocks.
#
# This ensures:
#   - minimal loss of reasoning continuity
#   - predictable memory reduction
#   - stable inference performance


# ============================================================
# SECTION 5 — HARD RESET
# ============================================================
# The engine supports a full reset operation that:
#   - clears compaction counters
#   - clears removal totals
#   - clears history logs
#
# This is used during:
#   - system stabilization cycles
#   - orchestrator resets
#   - major context corruption events


# ============================================================
# SECTION 6 — LOGGING & TELEMETRY
# ============================================================
# The engine logs:
#   - number of compactions
#   - total tokens removed
#   - timestamps
#
# HISTORY LIMIT:
#   - 200 entries
#
# Logs are consumed by:
#   - Stability & Recovery Engine
#   - System Orchestrator
#   - Meta‑Cognitive Reflection Engine


# ============================================================
# SECTION 7 — FAILURE MODES & RECOVERY
# ============================================================
# 1. CONTEXT OVERFLOW
#    - Context exceeds max_tokens.
#    - Recovery: compaction triggered.
#
# 2. AGGRESSIVE CUT TOO LOW
#    - Not enough tokens removed.
#    - Recovery: increase aggressive_cut.
#
# 3. CONTEXT CORRUPTION
#    - Blocks malformed or missing.
#    - Recovery: orchestrator triggers hard reset.


# ============================================================
# SECTION 8 — EXPANSION GUIDELINES
# ============================================================
# SAFE TO MODIFY:
#   - thresholds
#   - compaction strategy
#   - block prioritization rules
#
# DO NOT MODIFY:
#   - locking behavior
#   - logging structure
#   - snapshot format
#
# These components ensure deterministic behavior.


# ============================================================
# SECTION 9 — NOTES FOR TRUSTED ENGINEERS
# ============================================================
# - All modules must route context through this engine.
# - Never bypass compaction for "quick tests".
# - Thresholds must reflect real hardware limits.
# - Logs should be reviewed during stabilization cycles.
#
# This engine is mandatory for safe multi‑agent context management.
